notes:
Comparator lane balance, at zero rows. 1489.0 -> 1317.3 ticks, footprint unchanged at 14x14.
The asymmetry was never the W: a single d can only be entered on ONE heading, so one lane always
had to walk round the block's east side to reach it. d turns clockwise on BP > 0 and a turns
counter-clockwise, so each lane carries its OWN test and both deliver the man to the same entry
cell facing the same way. The block becomes a flattened figure-of-eight (body west along the
middle row, each lane east along its own) and the two test glyphs land in cells the old block was
already spending on transit. Same 7x3, both lanes 10 ticks.

Two coupled facts, neither safe alone (recorded in the docstring):
  - The caller now enters at the body's turn cell, SKIPPING the test. Sound only because the k==1
    pass no longer goes through the comparator at all — PASS routes it to its own emit lane.
  - X walked into heading WEST turns clockwise to the NORTH, so the positive lane is the top row.
    Getting this backwards is silent: loads, binds, runs, sorts, and emits the maximum.

Every s is now checked against lm.mjs route — the runtime's own nearest-pipe oracle — rather than
re-derived arithmetic, because two of the five clear their rival by exactly one cell (EMIT reaches
the output pipe at 7 against the ring's 8).

The bigger-output-pipe idea was measured and is dead: the worker's 6% stall is entirely the RING
((4,6) s 4.0%, (3,10) r 1.6%, (4,11) r 0.4%) and the output s has never blocked once, so the
ceiling is 0%, not 5%. The ring's own 4.0% is unreachable too — its forward pipe is 3 cells and
every westward route dead-ends against O's and I's south walls, where an arrowhead mints a phantom
outgoing pipe off an I/O room (a load error, not merely a wrong answer).

Also fixed this run: littleman/tools/heatmap.mjs now takes --expected/--frames and gates like
cmdJudge. It was passing "" to api.load, so it could not profile ANY round-gated machine — both
runners read as 97-99% stalled on s because the output room never drained. It now also ends the
sampling window at the settle tick rather than at --cap, and reports per-cell stall counts.

Previous: 465,680. This: 1.13x better.
